Implement user story AMSD-1820: [Mozio] - The Promo code amount is NOT displayed as expected for Return trip tickets in the Mozio email confirmation

## Story Description
[Mozio] - The Promo code amount is NOT displayed as expected for Return trip tickets in the Mozio email confirmation

## Acceptance Criteria
- Given a customer purchases a return trip ticket with a promo code applied, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the promo code discount amount is visible for both the outbound and return portions of the trip in the email body.
- Given a customer purchases a one-way trip ticket with a promo code applied, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the promo code discount amount visible in the email body equals the promo code discount amount applied to the booking.
- Given a return trip where the promo code discount exceeds the return fare portion, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the displayed promo code amount for the return portion does not exceed the return fare subtotal.
- Given a return trip ticket with a promo code applied, when the confirmation email is rendered in both HTML and plain text formats, then the promo code discount amount is present in both formats for the return leg.
- Given a return trip ticket with a promo code applied, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the promo code discount amount displayed for the outbound portion equals the promo code discount amount applied to the outbound portion of the booking.
- Given a return trip ticket with a promo code applied, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the sum of the promo code discount amounts displayed for the outbound and return portions equals the total promo code discount applied to the booking.
- Given a return trip ticket with a promo code applied, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the promo code discount amounts for both outbound and return portions are displayed in the same currency format and precision as other monetary values in the email.
- Given a return trip ticket with a promo code applied where the return fare portion is zero, when the Mozio confirmation email is generated, then the promo code discount amount displayed for the return portion is zero.


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/submit-reservations/apply-report-discounts.service.ts** (`applyReportDiscountsService`): This is the function that COMPUTES dispatch.report.price.discount (the promo amount Mozio renders in the confirmation email) by matching order line-item discounts to each dispatch's lineItems and accumulating the applied amount. The email template only READS report.price.discount; the wrong/missing return-leg value is produced here.
  - **Minimal fix:** The discount-to-dispatch match at lines 16-21 compares only `parseDispatchLineItemKey(lineItem.id).id === discount.lineItemId`, dropping the `isReturn` half of the key that the existing helper already returns. For return trips the outbound and return dispatch line items share the same base id, so the promo discount is matched/capped against the wrong leg (or not the return leg at all). Minimal change: destructure the full parse result — `const parsed = parseDispatchLineItemKey(lineItem.id)` — and match on both parts, e.g. `parsed.id === discount.lineItemId && parsed.isReturn === parseDispatchLineItemKey(discount.lineItemId).isReturn` (or compare against the correspondingly-keyed discount id for the return leg), so the discount is applied per-leg and capped by that leg's own selling price via the existing `Math.max(0, discountedAmount)` / `remainingDiscount` logic at lines 62-83, which already implements the 'discount does not exceed fare subtotal' rule once the correct leg is matched. Reuse the existing helper parseDispatchLineItemKey (already imported at line 5 from '../helpers/order'); do not write a new key parser.

